[
  {
    "lesson_id": "028",
    "trigger_word": "crime",
    "trigger_card_number": 601,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 28: EL PRESENT PERFECT",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "have/has + participio = el PRESENT PERFECT",
          "— una acción pasada con un pie en el",
          "ahora.",
          "",
          "  I have finished.                 Presiona 1",
          "  She has left.                    Presiona 2",
          "",
          "El participio es pariente de la forma",
          "de pasado:",
          "- muchas veces igual:  say → said → said",
          "- a veces una tercera forma:",
          "  go → went → gone",
          "  take → took → taken",
          "",
          "Aprende cada forma cuando la encuentres,",
          "nunca como una tabla de memoria."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I have finished.",
          "2": "She has left."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "CONTRACCIONES: I have → I've,",
          "she has → she's. Mismas palabras, rápido.",
          "",
          "Ojo: she's = she is O she has —",
          "solo la frase te dice cuál.",
          "She's happy. / She's gone home.",
          "",
          "El español tiene un tiempo igualito",
          "(he terminado): la estructura te ayuda.",
          "Pero en América el pasado de siempre es",
          "'terminé'. No uses el Present Perfect",
          "para todo lo pasado: en inglés ata la",
          "acción al momento de ahora."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "029",
    "trigger_word": "space",
    "trigger_card_number": 651,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 29: BEEN O GONE",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Dos participios de go, dos sentidos:",
          "",
          "BEEN = fue y ya volvió.",
          "  I have been to the bank. (ya volví)",
          "GONE = fue y todavía está lejos.",
          "  He has gone to the bank. (sigue fuera)",
          "",
          "\"Have you ever been to...?\" — así",
          "pregunta el inglés por una experiencia.",
          "",
          "En habla rápida el auxiliar se contrae:",
          "  I haven't been.                  Presiona 1",
          "  You've never been?               Presiona 2",
          "  He's gone out.                   Presiona 3",
          "  Haven't you gone yet?            Presiona 4"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I haven't been.",
          "2": "You've never been?",
          "3": "He's gone out.",
          "4": "Haven't you gone yet?"
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Las palabritas del Perfect, un uso cada una:",
          "",
          "ever    — ¿alguna vez? (en preguntas)",
          "never   — nunca, jamás",
          "already — antes de lo esperado (ya)",
          "yet     — hasta ahora (pregunta/negación)",
          "just    — hace un momento (recién)",
          "",
          "Casi todas van en medio, antes del",
          "participio: \"I have never seen it.\"",
          "Pero yet va al final:",
          "\"Have you finished yet?\"",
          "",
          "El español las coloca con más libertad —",
          "el inglés las fija: en medio, yet al final."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "030",
    "trigger_word": "test",
    "trigger_card_number": 676,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 30: PERFECT O PAST",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "La palabra de tiempo elige el tiempo verbal.",
          "",
          "TIEMPO CERRADO (terminó) → past simple:",
          "yesterday, last week, ago, in 2019",
          "",
          "  I saw the film yesterday.        Presiona 1",
          "  She called twice last week.      Presiona 2",
          "  We spoke two days ago.           Presiona 3",
          "",
          "TIEMPO ABIERTO (sigue) → present perfect:",
          "today, this week, ever, so far",
          "",
          "  I have seen the film this week.  Presiona 4",
          "  She has called twice today.      Presiona 5",
          "  So far we have spoken twice.     Presiona 6"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I saw the film yesterday.",
          "2": "She called twice last week.",
          "3": "We spoke two days ago.",
          "4": "I have seen the film this week.",
          "5": "She has called twice today.",
          "6": "So far we have spoken twice."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "FOR y SINCE miden el tiempo abierto:",
          "for + duración:  \"for ten years\"",
          "since + inicio:  \"since Monday\"",
          "",
          "En español: durante/por (for) y desde",
          "(since) — el inglés usa dos palabras fijas.",
          "",
          "Misma acción, dos tiempos:",
          "\"I lived here for years.\" (terminó)",
          "\"I have lived here for years.\" (sigue)",
          "",
          "En América el pasado simple manda mucho.",
          "En inglés la palabra de tiempo decide:",
          "léela primero."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "031",
    "trigger_word": "art",
    "trigger_card_number": 701,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 31: USED TO (SOLÍA)",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "used to + verbo base = una costumbre del",
          "pasado que ya terminó. El verbo no cambia.",
          "",
          "  I used to work here.             Presiona 1",
          "  (Yo trabajaba aquí. / Yo solía trabajar",
          "   aquí.)",
          "",
          "  She used to play every day.      Presiona 2",
          "",
          "PREGUNTA y NEGACIÓN con el motor do",
          "(como did) — used pasa a use:",
          "",
          "  Did you use to live here?        Presiona 3",
          "",
          "  I didn't use to like it.         Presiona 4"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I used to work here.",
          "2": "She used to play every day.",
          "3": "Did you use to live here?",
          "4": "I didn't use to like it."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "No confundas dos formas parecidas:",
          "",
          "  used to + verbo = una costumbre",
          "  pasada (ya terminó):",
          "     I used to have long hair.     Presiona 1",
          "     (ya no)",
          "",
          "  be used to + algo = estar acostumbrado:",
          "     I am used to the cold.        Presiona 2",
          "     (Estoy acostumbrado al frío.)",
          "",
          "El español calca esto: used to va con",
          "el imperfecto (trabajaba) o con",
          "'solía + infinitivo' (solía trabajar)."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I used to have long hair.",
          "2": "I am used to the cold."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "032",
    "trigger_word": "carry",
    "trigger_card_number": 754,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 32: WAS DOING (ESCENA)",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "was / were + verbo-ing = una escena que",
          "transcurría en el pasado.",
          "",
          "  She was sleeping.  (Ella dormía.)",
          "  We were carrying the boxes.      Presiona 1",
          "",
          "Es la misma forma -ing de 'ahora', pero",
          "con el pasado de be:",
          "",
          "  I / he / she / it  was",
          "  you / we / they     were"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "She was sleeping.",
          "2": "We were carrying the boxes."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Dos tiempos cuentan la historia juntos:",
          "",
          "  la escena larga = was/were + -ing",
          "  el evento breve = past simple",
          "",
          "  I was carrying the box when I fell.",
          "  escena: was carrying / evento: fell",
          "",
          "El español calca esto bien: la escena va",
          "en imperfecto o 'estaba + -ndo', y el",
          "evento en pretérito. while = mientras,",
          "when = cuando:",
          "  Mientras ella cocinaba, sonó el teléfono."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I was carrying the box when I fell."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
]